ponents\n\n### 1.1 Data Engineering Preprocessing Pipeline\n\nBefore the model, a critical preprocessing stage ensures data quality:\n\n| Stage | Method | Justification |\n|-------|--------|---------------|\n| QC | MAD-based outlier detection (3 MADs on n_genes, mito%) | Robust for scRNA-seq noise |\n| Normalization | SCTransform v2 (Pearson residuals) | Stabilizes variance, handles sparsity better than log-norm |\n| Feature Selection | HVG (2000) \u222a Pathway genes \u222a CRISPRi targets | Preserves biological signal |\n| Batch Correction | Harmony on cell-cycle + donor | Prevents confounding |\n| Sparse Storage | CSR matrices, `anndata` backed mode | Memory efficiency (10\u2074 cells \u00d7 10\u00b3 genes) |\n\n**Key Engineering Decision**: Store expression as **sparse CSR** throughout; convert to dense only in mini-batches (batch_size=256).\n\n### 1.2 Core Architecture: PerturbNet-KO\n\n```\nInput: (X_control, g_perturb, cell_context)\n   \u2502\n   \u251c\u2500\u2500\u25ba [1] Gene Embedding Layer (E_g: 200\nSingle Cell Biology Expert: # PerturbNet-KO: Biologically-Grounded Architecture for CRISPRi Gene Knockout Prediction\n\n## Executive Summary\n\nI propose **PerturbNet-KO**, a **mechanistic-aware hybrid architecture** that combines a **Gene Regulatory Graph Transformer (GRGT)** with a **Conditional Variational Autoencoder (cVAE)** and a **Biologically-Informed Residual Decoder**. The core innovation is a **two-pathway design** that explicitly separates **direct CRISPRi knockdown effects** (target gene \u2192 downstream cascade) from **compensatory network responses**, both grounded in a **prior-regularized gene regulatory graph** derived from K562-specific data (ENCODE, STRING, and perturbation databases like Perturb-seq references).\n\nThis design directly addresses the three hardest challenges in CRISPRi knockout prediction:\n1. **Data sparsity** (few cells per perturbation) \u2192 hierarchical Bayesian shrinkage via cVAE\n2. **Unseen perturbation generalization** \u2192 graph-based message passing over the regulatory network\n3. **Biological interpretability** \u2192 explicit pathway-level latent variables and attention weights that map to known regulatory edges\n\n---\n\n## 1. Architecture Components\n\n### 1.1 Overall Data Flow\n\n```\nInputs:\n  X_ctrl \u2208 \u211d^(N\u00d7G)     (control expression, sparse)\n  g_target \u2208 {0,1}^G    (one-hot CRISPRi target)\n  c_context \u2208 \u211d^K       (cell-cycle, donor, batch embeddings)\n\n                    \u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510\n                    \u2502  1. Gene Embedding + GRGT       \u2502\n                    \u2502     (Regulatory Graph Transformer)\u2502\n                    \u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u252c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\n                                   \u2502\nData Engineer: # Data Engineering Feedback on PerturbNet-KO\n\n## 1. Technical Analysis\n\n### 1.1 Architectural Design Evaluation\n\n**Strengths:**\n- The GRGT + cVAE + Residual Decoder combination is well-motivated. The two-pathway separation (direct knockdown vs. compensatory) is a genuine architectural innovation that maps to biology.\n- Using CSR sparse storage with dense mini-batch conversion (batch=256) is appropriate for 10\u2074 cells \u00d7 10\u00b3 genes; peak dense memory \u2248 256 \u00d7 2000 \u00d7 4 bytes \u2248 2 MB per batch, negligible.\n\n**Critical Concerns:**\n\n**(a) Graph Transformer scaling.** Full self-attention over G=2000 gene nodes is O(G\u00b2\u00b7d) = 4M\u00b7d per layer. With d=256 and 6 layers, this is ~6 GB activations per batch at fp32. **Recommendation:** Use **sparse graph attention** restricted to the regulato