ization** \u2192 graph-based message passing over the regulatory network\n3. **Biological interpretability** \u2192 explicit pathway-level latent variables and attention weights that map to known regulatory edges\n\n---\n\n## 1. Architecture Components\n\n### 1.1 Overall Data Flow\n\n```\nInputs:\n  X_ctrl \u2208 \u211d^(N\u00d7G)     (control expression, sparse)\n  g_target \u2208 {0,1}^G    (one-hot CRISPRi target)\n  c_context \u2208 \u211d^K       (cell-cycle, donor, batch embeddings)\n\n                    \u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510\n                    \u2502  1. Gene Embedding + GRGT       \u2502\n                    \u2502     (Regulatory Graph Transformer)\u2502\n                    \u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u252c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\n                                   \u2502\nData Engineer: # Data Engineering Feedback on PerturbNet-KO\n\n## 1. Technical Analysis\n\n### 1.1 Architectural Design Evaluation\n\n**Strengths:**\n- The GRGT + cVAE + Residual Decoder combination is well-motivated. The two-pathway separation (direct knockdown vs. compensatory) is a genuine architectural innovation that maps to biology.\n- Using CSR sparse storage with dense mini-batch conversion (batch=256) is appropriate for 10\u2074 cells \u00d7 10\u00b3 genes; peak dense memory \u2248 256 \u00d7 2000 \u00d7 4 bytes \u2248 2 MB per batch, negligible.\n\n**Critical Concerns:**\n\n**(a) Graph Transformer scaling.** Full self-attention over G=2000 gene nodes is O(G\u00b2\u00b7d) = 4M\u00b7d per layer. With d=256 and 6 layers, this is ~6 GB activations per batch at fp32. **Recommendation:** Use **sparse graph attention** restricted to the regulatory adjacency (ENCODE/STRING edges, ~50K edges for 2000 genes), reducing complexity to O(|E|\u00b7d). This is both faster and biologically correct \u2014 genes should attend only to regulatory neighbors, not all genes.\n\n**(b) cVAE posterior collapse risk.** With ~200 perturbations \u00d7 ~50 cells each, the decoder can ignore z and reconstruct from g_target alone. **Recommendation:** Use **\u03b2-annealing** (\u03b2 from 0\u21920.1 over first 20 epochs) plus **free-bits** (\u22650.5 nats/dim) to prevent collapse. Also KL-weight per perturbation rather than per cell.\n\n**(c) Residual decoder target.** The proposal doesn't specify whether\nSingle Cell Biology Expert: # Single Cell Biology Expert Feedback on PerturbNet-KO\n\n## Executive Summary\n\nPerturbNet-KO is a well-conceived architecture, but from a **single-cell biology perspective**, the current design has critical gaps that will limit its ability to beat SOTA on CRISPRi knockout prediction in K562. The most consequential issues are: (1) **CRISPRi is not a knockout** \u2014 it's a partial knockdown with variable efficiency, and the model treats it as binary; (2) **K562-specific biology is under-exploited** (erythroleukemic lineage, near-haploid genome, well-characterized CML regulatory network); (3) **the two-pathway design is biologically correct but under-specified** \u2014 it lacks proper causal structure for direct vs. compensatory effects; (4) **evaluation metrics chosen (MSE/PCC/R2) will reward mean-collapse** \u2014 the model needs architectural pressure toward differential expression. Below I provide detailed, actionable feedback.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Technical Analysis\n\n### 1.1 Architectural Design Evaluation\n\n**Strengths:**\n- Two-pathway (direct + compensatory) separation is genuinely aligned with CRISPRi biology \u2014 direct knockdown effects are near-deterministic for the target, while downstream effects are network-mediated and heterogeneous.\n- Graph Transformer with regulatory edges is the right inductive bias; K562 has one of the best-characterized GRNs (ENCODE, ChIP-seq for ~200 TFs, Perturb-seq references).\n\n**Critical Concerns:**\n\n**(a) Binary perturbation encoding is biologically wrong for CRISPRi.** CRISPRi achieves ~70\u201395% knockdown, not 100% knockout, and efficiency varies by sgRNA/target. Encoding\nData Engineer: # Data Engineering Critical Questions for PerturbNet-KO\n\nBuilding on the exchange so far, I want to pressure-test the design from a **data engineering** standpoint \u2014 specifically around the interfaces between preprocessing, the GRGT, the cVAE, and the residual decoder. Several of the earlier concerns (sparse attention, posterior collapse, CRISPRi efficiency encoding) create downstream data-flow obligations that haven't been resolved. Below are the questions I believe must be answered before we commit to an implementation.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Technical Clarifications \u2014 Data Flow & Tensor Contracts\n\n**Q1.1 \u2014 What is the exact tensor contract between the GRGT and the cVAE?**\nThe GRGT produces per-gene embeddings `H \u2208 \u211d^(G\u00d7d)`. Does the cVAE encoder consume:\n- (a) the **full** `H` (G\u00d7d \u2014 2000\u00d7256 \u2248 0.5M floats per cell),\n- (b) a **pooled** representation (e.g., mean over genes \u2192 d-dim), or\n- (c) a **perturbation-conditioned slice** (embedding of `g_target` only)?\n\nThis choice determines whether the cVAE can model *heterogeneous* responses across cells or collapses to a per-perturbation mean. Given the evaluation rewards PCC/R\u00b2 (per-gene correlations), option (c) alone will fail. I recommend **(a) with a learned attention pooling weighted by the target gene's neighborhood** \u2014 but this needs to be specified, and the memory budget at batch=256 must be recomputed.\n\n**Q1.2 \u2014 How are the \"two pathways\" actually separated in the decoder?**\nThe\n\n\nDOMAIN CONTEXT:\n\nKey Biological Considerations:\n1. Cell Type Specificity:\n   - Cell type-specific response patterns\n   - Lineage relationships and developmental trajectories\n   - Cell state transitions and plasticity\n   - Tissue-specific regulatory networks\n\n2. Perturbation Response:\n   - Direct vs indirect effects\n   - Temporal dynamics of responses\n   - Compensatory mechanisms\n   - Pathway cross-talk\n\n3. Regulatory Networks:\n   - Transcription factor networks\n   - Signaling cascades\n   - Metabolic pathways\n   - Gene regulatory circuits\n\n4. Quality Control:\n   - Technical vs biological variation\n   - Batch effects and normalization\n   - Cell cycle effects\n   - Dropout patterns"
