learned attention pooling weighted by the target gene's neighborhood** \u2014 but this needs to be specified, and the memory budget at batch=256 must be recomputed.\n\n**Q1.2 \u2014 How are the \"two pathways\" actually separated in the decoder?**\nThe\nSingle Cell Biology Expert: # PerturbNet-KO v2: Response to Data Engineer's Critical Questions\n\n## Executive Summary\n\nThe Data Engineer's questions expose a real risk: the current design under-specifies the **tensor contracts** between GRGT, cVAE, and decoder, and this ambiguity could silently collapse the model into a per-perturbation mean predictor \u2014 which would fail PCC/R\u00b2. I now provide **explicit tensor contracts**, a **revised two-pathway decoder with proper causal structure**, and a **CRISPRi-efficiency-aware perturbation encoding** that replaces the binary one-hot. I also address the biological concerns raised earlier (partial knockdown, K562-specific biology, mean-collapse pressure).\n\n---\n\n## 1. Answering Q1.1 \u2014 GRGT \u2194 cVAE Tensor Contract\n\n**Recommendation: option (a) with target-neighborhood attention pooling \u2014 but with a critical modification to avoid memory blowup.**\n\n### 1.1 Revised GRGT output\n\nThe GRGT does **not** produce a full `H \u2208 \u211d^(G\u00d7d)` per cell. Instead, it produces a **query-conditioned neighborhood embedding**:\n\n$$\nH_q = \\text{GRGT}(X_{\\text{ctrl}}, \\mathcal{G}) \\in \\mathbb{R}^{G \\times d}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathcal{G}$ is the K562 regulatory graph (sparse, ~50K edges). Because we use **sparse graph attention** (as the Data Engineer recommended), $H_q$ is computed only over the 2-hop neighborhood of the target gene `g_target` \u2014 call this neighborhood $\\mathcal{N}(g) \\subseteq V$, with $|\\mathcal{N}(g)| \\approx 50\n\n\nDOMAIN CONTEXT:\nNo specific domain context available for this expert type.\nAs a scientific critic, provide a comprehensive evaluation of the current proposals:\n\n1. Scientific Merit (Score 0.1-1.0):\n   - T