's expression, (b) the downstream response is *propagated through a learned K562-specific GRN with proper causal masking*, and (c) cell-level heterogeneity is *preserved by construction* through a perturbation-conditioned latent that is architecturally forbidden from collapsing to a per-perturbation mean.\n\nThe three structural risks flagged by the Critic are resolved explicitly:\n1. **Mean-collapse** \u2192 resolved via a **per-gene Pearson auxiliary loss** + **variance-preserving latent regularization** + **cell-level InfoNCE** (Section 3.3).\n2. **Two-pathway causal separation** \u2192 resolved via **orthogonal subspace projection** + **pathway-specific supervision heads** + **gradient isolation** (Section 1.4).\n3. **CRISPRi efficiency encoding** \u2192 resolved via **continuous efficiency scalar injected as FiLM modulation** at three sites, with a **sequence-based efficiency predictor** for unseen sgRNAs (Section 1.2).\n\n---\n\n## 1. Architecture Components\n\n### 1.1 Component Overview (Data Flow)\n\n```\nraw counts \u2500\u2500\u25ba [QC +\nData Engineer: # Data Engineer Feedback: PerturbNet-KO v4 \u2014 Technical Review & Path to SOTA\n\n## Executive Assessment\n\nv4 is architecturally sound and biologically well-motivated, but as a **data engineer I must flag that the proposal is under-specified on the data-flow contracts that determine whether the architecture can actually be trained on `cellforge_input` at scale**. The single-cell biology framing is excellent; the engineering scaffolding to realize it is incomplete. Below I give a structured critique with concrete, actionable fixes \u2014 many of which are the difference between \"beats baseline on paper\" and \"beats baseline on the eval harness (MSE / PCC / R\u00b2).\"\n\n---\n\n## 1. Technical Analysis\n\n### 1.1 Architectural design \u2014 concerns\n\n| Issue | Severity | Concrete Fix |\n|---|---|---|\n| **GRN adjacency is unspecified in provenance.** v4 says \"learned K562-specific GRN\" but doesn't say *initialized from what*. A randomly-initialized dense G\u00d7G adjacency (G\u22482000 HVGs \u2192 4M params) will overfit under data sparsity. | **High** | Initialize from a **prior GRN** (e.g., K562-specific ChIP-Atlas / ENCODE / scMTNI), keep only edges with prior support, and learn a **residual adjacency** `A = A_prior \u2299 M + \u0394A` where `M` is a learnable mask. This is the single biggest lever for generalization to unseen perturbations. |\n| **Neighborhood-restricted query embedding** (Q1.1) is good for memory but the *neighborhood definition* is a data contract. | Medium | Define\nSingle Cell Biology Expert: # Single Cell Biology Expert Feedback: PerturbNet-KO v4 \u2192 v5\n\n## Preamble: What the Biology Demands That the Architecture Still Misses\n\nThe Data Engineer and Critic have done an excellent job closing the **engineering contracts** (GRN provenance, neighborhood definition, efficiency encoding). My job here is to evaluate v4 through the lens of **what K562 CRISPRi biology actually looks like** and where the architecture is *biologically* under-specified in ways that will directly cost MSE/PCC/R\u00b2 on the eval harness.\n\nThree biological gaps in v4 that I consider **performance-critical** (not just \"nice to have\"):\n\n1. **K562 is a highly aneuploid, near-triploid CML line** \u2014 copy-number heterogeneity means the *same* sgRNA produces different knockdown efficiencies across subclones, and many \"knockouts\" are partial. The architecture treats efficiency as a *scalar per sgRNA*, but biologically it is a **cell \u00d7 sgRNA interaction**. This is a direct source of unexplained variance that will cap PCC.\n2. **CRISPRi is not knockout \u2014 it is transcriptional repression with off-target and position effects**. The direct-effect head assumes the target gene goes to ~0; in K562 CRISPRi data (Replogle et al. 2022), median knockdown is ~70\u201385%, with a heavy tail of inefficient guides. The architecture must model **partial repression as a distribution**, not a point.\n3. **Compensatory response is cell-state-dependent and temporally structured** \u2014 K562 cells under CRISPRi show strong stress/apoptosis signatures at later timepoints and cell-cycle-dependent buffering. The v4 \"\nData Engineer: # Data Engineer: Critical Questions to Drive v5 to SOTA\n\nThe v4/v5 dialogue has been productive, but as the data engineer responsible for the **train/eval pipeline that actually produces MSE/PCC/R\u00b2**, I need to pin down contracts that will determine whether we *hit* SOTA or merely *describe* it. I'll organize by the five axes, then close with the specific data-flow contracts I consider **blocking**.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Technical Clarifications\n\n**Q1.1 \u2014 GRN prior provenance & sparsity budget.**\nThe Critic's fix (`A = A_prior \u2299 M + \u0394A`) is correct in spirit but under-specified as a data contract. I need answers to:\n- **Which prior, at what edge density?** ChIP-Atlas K562 gives ~10\u2075 TF\u2192gene edges over ~20k genes; scMTNI is sparser. On 2,000 HVGs, what fraction of the G\u00d7G matrix is non-zero after projection? If `<1%`, a sparse `torch.sparse` residual `\u0394A` is mandatory; if `>10%`, dense is fine. **This determines memory layout.**\n- **How do we handle genes with *zero* prior edges** (many HVGs will be orphaned)? Fallback to a learned \"global\" adjacency, or a kNN-in-expression-graph prior? Orphan handling will directly affect PCC on the long tail of low-expression genes.\n\n**Q1.2 \u2014 Neighborhood definition for `H_q \u2208 \u211d^{|N(g)|\u00d7d}`.**\n\"Neighborhood\" is the load-bearing contract. Candidates: (a)\nSingle Cell Biology Expert: # PerturbNet-KO v5: A Biologically-Grounded, Engineering-Complete Architecture for CRISPRi Knockout Prediction in K562\n\n## Executive Summary\n\nv5 resolves the three **biological gaps** flagged by the Biology Expert (cell\u00d7sgRNA efficiency heterogeneity, partial-repression-as-distribution, cell-state-dependent compensation) *and* the five **blocking data-flow contracts** flagged by the Data Engineer (GRN provenance/sparsity, neighborhood definition, efficiency predictor training signal, cell-state conditioning, and eval-harness alignment). The organizing principle is now sharper than v4:\n\n> **CRISPRi knockout prediction in K562 = a *partially-identified counterfactual* over a *sparse, prior-anchored GRN*, where the target gene's post-perturbation state is a *distribution* (not a point), and downstream propagation is *modulated by cell state* (cycle, subclone, stress) that must be inferred, not averaged away.**\n\nEvery architectural choice below traces back to a measurable effect on MSE / PCC / R\u00b2 and to a biological fact about K562 CRISPRi (Replogle 2022, Norman 2019, Adamson 2016).\n\n---\n\n## 1. Resolving the Blocking Contracts (v4 \u2192 v5)\n\n### 1.1 GRN provenance & sparsity budget (Data Engineer Q1.1)\n\n**Adopted contract:**\n- **Primary prior:** ChIP-Atlas K562 TF\u2192gene edges, filtered to `score \u2265 500` (strong binding), projected onto the 2,000 HVG panel via gene-symbol mapping. Expected edge density on HVG panel: **~2\n\n\nDOMAIN CONTEXT:\nNo specific domain context available for this expert type.\nAs a scientific critic, provide a comprehensive evaluation of the current proposals:\n\n1. Scientific Merit (Score 0.1-1.0):\n   - Theoretical foundation\n   - Methodological rigor\n   - Innovation level\n   - Technical sophistication\n   - Biological relevance\n\n2. Technical Feasibility (Score 0.1-1.0):\n   - Implementation complexity\n   - Resource requirements\n   - Scalability potential\n   - Performance expectations\n   - Maintenance considerations\n\n3. Biological Validity (Score 0.1-1.0):\n   - Cell type handling\n   - Pathway integration\n   - Perturbation modeling\n   - Constraint compliance\n   - Interpretability\n\n4. SOTA Comparison (Score 0.1-1.0):\n   - Performance potential\n   - Innovation aspects\n   - Technical advantages\n   - Biological accuracy\n   - Overall competitiveness\n\n5. Implementation Strategy (Score 0.1-1.0):\n   - Development approach\n   - Resource allocation\n   - Timeline realism\n   - Risk management\n   - Quality assurance\n\nProvide a detailed justification for each score and an overall score (0.1-1.0) weighted across all categories.\nFocus on potential for surpassing SOTA performance in single-cell perturbation prediction."
