racts, feature-engineering pipeline, and training regime** that make each architectural component *measurably* load-bearing \u2014 and to prune components that cannot demonstrate a delta under the `cellforge_input` compute/sparsity budget.\n\nThe organizing principle for v6:\n\n> **Every architectural component must be tied to (a) a specific data artifact produced by the pipeline, (b) a specific failure mode of baselines on K562 CRISPRi, and (c) an ablation that can falsify its contribution within the eval budget.**\n\nI will not re-litigate biology (settled in v5) or the theoretical framing (settled by the Critic). I will specify the **engineering contracts** that make v5 trainable and SOTA-beating.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Architecture Components\n\n### 1.0 Data Pipeline Contract (upstream of every component)\n\nBefore any component, the pipeline must produce a **frozen, versioned artifact bundle** that every downstream component consumes. This is the single most important engineering decision \u2014 it prevents train/eval leakage and makes ablations reproducible.\n\n| Artifact | Format | Specification | Consumer |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `X_base` | CSR sparse, `float32` | log1p-C\n\n\nDOMAIN CONTEXT:\n\nKey Biological Considerations:\n1. Cell Type Specificity:\n   - Cell type-specific response patterns\n   - Lineage relationships and developmental trajectories\n   - Cell state transitions and plasticity\n   - Tissue-specific regulatory networks\n\n2. Perturbation Response:\n   - Direct vs indirect effects\n   - Temporal dynamics of responses\n   - Compensatory mechanisms\n   - Pathway cross-talk\n\n3. Regulatory Networks:\n   - Transcription factor networks\n   - Signaling cascades\n   - Metabolic pathways\n   - Gene regulatory circuits\n\n4. Quality Control:\n   - Technical vs biological variation\n   - Batch effects and normalization\n   - Cell cycle effects\n   - Dropout patterns\nPlease propose a detailed architectural solution focusing on your domain expertise. Your pr