cle scores are inputs, but the architecture does not make downstream propagation *depend* on cell state. This is the single largest PCC gap vs. SOTA (GEARS, scGPT-perturb, CPA).\n3. **The GRN prior is treated as a static adjacency, but the biology requires *edge-level sign and confidence* and *pathway-level aggregation*** \u2014 without this, the model cannot distinguish direct CRISPRi effects from compensatory indirect effects, which is exactly what R\u00b2 rewards on held-out perturbations.\n\nBelow I give specific, falsifiable biological critiques and the v6.2 specification that closes them.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Technical Analysis\n\n### 1.1 Architectural Design Evaluation\nData Engineer: # Data Engineer's Critical Questions & v6.3 Engineering Interrogation\n\n## Executive Framing\n\nThe v6/v6.1/v6.2 proposals now cover biology, engineering, and mechanism. My job as Data Engineer is to **stress-test whether the specified data contracts actually deliver the deltas they promise** on `cellforge_input`. I will not re-argue settled points. I will ask the questions that, if unanswered, will cause v6 to underperform GEARS/CPA/scGPT-perturb on the eval harness \u2014 and I will propose the concrete engineering resolutions inline so the discussion converges.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Technical Clarifications (Data-Flow & Contracts)\n\n### Q1.1 \u2014 Where exactly is the train/val/test split boundary, and is it perturbation-disjoint *and* guide-disjoint?\n\nThe proposals say \"held-out perturbations\" but never specify the split unit. On `cellforge_input` (Replogle-derived K562 CRISPRi), the split unit matters enormously:\n\n- **Perturbation-disjoint** (target gene held out): tests generalization to unseen targets \u2014 this is what PCC/R\u00b2 on \"unseen perturbations\" means.\n- **Guide-disjoint** (some guides of a *seen* target held out): tests guide-averaging robustness \u2014 a *different* generalization axis.\n- **Cell-state-disjoint**: not meaningful here (K562 is one line), but **batch-disjoint** (Replogle batch 1 vs. 2) is.\n\n**Engineering resolution I propose:** a **three-way frozen split manifest** (`split_v1.parquet`) with columns `(cell_id, target_gene, guide_id, batch_id\nSingle Cell Biology Expert: # PerturbNet-KO v7: A Biologically-Resolved, Falsifiable Architecture for CRISPRi Knockout Prediction in K562\n\n## Preamble: What v7 Resolves\n\nThe v6\u2192v6.3 dialogue converged on *engineering discipline* (frozen artifacts, ablation budgets, split manifests) but the Data Engineer's Q1.1\u2013Q1.3 remain unanswered, and the Critic's core demand \u2014 **every component earns its delta** \u2014 has been answered *mechanically* but not *mechanistically*. As the Single Cell Biology Expert, my contribution to v7 is to close the **biological-mechanism \u2192 measurable-delta** loop for the three components that actually drive PCC/R\u00b2 on K562 CRISPRi, and to **explicitly retire** components that cannot be biologically justified under the `cellforge_input` sparsity budget.\n\nThe organizing biological principle, refined from v6.2:\n\n> **CRISPRi knockdown in K562 is a *partial, cell-state-conditional repression* whose downstream signature is dominated by *transcriptional buffering* (target-class-specific), *compensatory pathway activation*, and *cell-cycle-gated propagation*. A model that cannot represent \"the cell compensates and expression does not change\" will systematically over-predict effect size and lose PCC on held-out targets \u2014 this is the single largest gap vs. GEARS/CPA on Replogle 2022.**\n\nEvery component below is paired with: **(a)** the K562 biology, **(b)** the baseline failure mode it corrects, **(c)** the ablation that falsifies it, and **(d)** the data contract from v6.1 it consumes.\n\n---\n\n##\nCritic: # Critic's Comprehensive Evaluation: PerturbNet-KO v7 Proposal Series\n\n## Executive Summary\n\nThe v6\u2192v7 dialogue has produced an architecture that is **substantially more rigorous than the current S