** \u2014 the actual tensor operations that encode the biology \u2014 while **consuming v8's data contracts** as the input specification and **honoring v7's falsifiability discipline** as the validation harness.\n\nThe organizing principle (unchanged, refined):\n\n> **CRISPRi knockdown in K562 is a *partial, cell-state-conditional repression* whose downstream signature is dominated by *transcriptional buffering* (target-class-specific), *compensatory pathway activation*, and *cell-cycle-gated propagation*. The model must be able to *learn \"no change\" as an outcome*, must *gate propagation on cell state*, and must *distinguish direct from compensatory indirect effects via signed, confidence-weighted GRN edges*.**\n\nv9 makes this concrete as **five coupled modules**, each with a specified tensor operation, a specified data contract from v8, and a specified falsification test from v7. I explicitly **retire** the following components from earlier proposals because they cannot be biologically justified under the `cellforge_input`\nData Engineer: # Data Engineer's Review of PerturbNet-KO v8/v9: From Contracts to Competitive Performance\n\n## Executive Summary\n\nv8 and v9 have each done serious work \u2014 v8 materialized the data contracts, v9 specified the biological mechanism. But from a **data-engineering standpoint**, both proposals still under-specify the three things that actually determine whether we *beat* SOTA (MSE/PCC/R\u00b2) rather than *match* it:\n\n1. **The target tensor itself is under-specified.** Neither proposal fully commits to how `cellforge_input` becomes a `(perturbation \u00d7 gene)` target matrix with a variance/confidence mask. This is where PCC is won or lost.\n2. **The conditioning embedding is described biologically but not statistically.** \"Batch-invariant learned embedding\" is a goal, not an operation. Without an explicit adversarial or residualization step, batch leaks into the perturbation embedding and R\u00b2 collapses on held-out perturbations.\n3. **The GRN prior is invoked as signed/confidence-weighted but never materialized at K562 scale.** A dense 10k\u00d710k signed operator is 800 MB in fp64 and ~400 MB fp32 per forward pass \u2014 computationally infeasible as described. This must be a sparse, chunked, block-structured operator or the proposal is unimplementable.\n\nBelow I give a **component-by-component technical review**, then a **concrete revised architecture (v10)** with the tensor contracts v8 promised but did not fully deliver.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Technical Analysis\n\n### 1.1 Architectural Design Evaluation\n\n**Strengths (v8):**\n- The three-layer decomposition (target construction \u2192 conditioning embedding \u2192 GR\nSingle Cell Biology Expert: # Single Cell Biology Expert's Review of PerturbNet-KO v8/v9/v10 Dialogue\n\n## Executive Summary\n\nThe v6\u2192v10 dialogue has produced genuine progress at three layers \u2014 biological mechanism (v7), falsifiability (v6.1), data contracts (v8), and now the Data Engineer's v10 push toward materialized tensors. My role as Single Cell Biology Expert is to evaluate whether the **specified computational mechanism actually captures the biology of CRISPRi knockdown in K562** at the level that determines MSE/PCC/R\u00b2, and to identify where the architecture is *biologically mis-specified* in ways that will cap performance regardless of engineering quality.\n\nMy central verdict: **v10's data-engineering discipline is necessary but not sufficient.** The architecture still under-specifies four *biological* mechanisms that dominate the residual variance in Replogle-derived K562 CRISPRi data:\n\n1. **The target-class-dependent buffering regime** (essential vs. non-essential genes have qualitatively different response distributions \u2014 a single regression head cannot fit both).\n2. **The cell-state gating of propagation** (K562 is a leukemia line with a strong cell-cycle and erythroid-differentiation axis; perturbation effects are gated on this latent state).\n3. **The direct-vs-compensatory decomposition** (PCC is dominated by the *direct* effect; R\u00b2 is dominated by *compensatory* off-target network response \u2014 these require separate heads).\n4. **The \"no-change\" mass** (most knockdowns produce near-zero expression change; MSE is dominated by correctly predicting null, not by predicting large effects).\n\nI provide a **biologically-resolved v11 specification\nData Engineer: # Data Engineer's Critical Questions: From v10/v11 Specification to SOTA-Surpassing Execution\n\n## Framing\n\nv8 materialized contracts, v9 specified mechanism, v10 pushed tensors, v11 added biological resolution. The dialogue has converged on *what to build*. My questions now target the **gap between specification and executable performance** \u2014 the places where, in my experience shipping perturbation models, the design looks complete on paper but loses 15\u201330% of achievable PCC/R\u00b2 in implementation. I organize these as **blocking questions** (must resolve before coding) and **sharpening questions** (resolve during iteration).\n\n---\n\n## 1. The Target Tensor: Where PCC Is Actually Won or Lost\n\nThe proposals agree on `(perturbation \u00d7 gene)` targets with confidence masks but never commit to the **estimator** that produces them. This is not a detail \u2014 it is the dominant variance source on Replogle-derived K562.\n\n**Q1.1 \u2014 Guide-averaging vs. guide-pooling.** Replogle CRISPRi has 2\u20134 guides per target with *systematically different* on-target efficiencies. Do we (a) average per-cell expression across guides, (b) treat each guide as a separate perturbation and learn a guide-effect latent, or (c) fit a hierarchical model where guide efficiency is a learned scalar per guide? Option (a) collapses real heterogeneity and biases toward the most-efficient guide; (c) is the biologically correct choice but requires the model to *condition on guide identity at train time and marginalize at test time*. Which does v11 commit to, and how does the test-time marginalization affect R\u00b2 on held-out perturbations\nSingle Cell Biology Expert: # Single Cell Biology Expert's Response: The Biological Estimator Specification for PerturbNet-KO v12\n\n## Framing: Answering the Data Engineer's Blocking Questions\n\nThe Data Engineer's Q1.1\u2013Q1.x are the right questions. They are not implementation details \u2014 they are **biological decisions that determine the target tensor**, and the target tensor determines the achievable PCC ceiling. I answer them here with explicit biological justification, then specify the **v12 additions** that close the remaining biological gaps from v11.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Blocking Answers: The Target Tensor Estimator\n\n### Q1.1 \u2014 Guide-averaging vs. pooling vs. hierarchical: **Hierarchical, with test-time marginalization**\n\n**Biological justification.** Replogle CRISPRi guides against the same target have systematically different on-target efficiencies (typically 40\u201390% knockdown) because they hit different protospacer positions with different chromatin accessibility and different off-target profiles. This is *not* noise \u2014 it is a **measured biological quantity** (guide efficiency correlates with position, GC content, and local chromatin state).\n\n**The three options are not equivalent:**\n\n| Option | What it does | Biological failure mode |\n|---|---|---|\n| (a) Guide averaging | Collapses to per-target mean | **Biases toward the most-efficient guide.** If guide 1 gives 85% KD and guide 2 gives 40% KD, averaging gives a response that *no real guide produces*. PCC against held-out guides will be systematically low because the test guide is a *sample* from a distribution the model never saw. |\n| (b) Guide as separate perturbation | Learns guide-effect latent | **Cannot generalize to unseen targets\n\n\nDOMAIN CONTEXT:\nNo specific domain context available for this expert type.\nAs a scientific critic, provide a comprehensive evaluation of the current proposals:\n\n1. Scientific Merit (Score 0.1-1.0):\n   - Theoretical foundation\n   - Methodological rigor\n   - Innovation level\n   - Technical sophistication\n   - Biological relevance\n\n2. Technical Feasibility (Score 0.1-1.0):\n   - Implementation complexity\n   - Resource requirements\n   - Scalability potential\n   - Performance expectations\n   - Maintenance considerations\n\n3. Biological Validity (Score 0.1-1.0):\n   - Cell type handling\n   - Pathway integration\n   - Perturbation modeling\n   - Constraint compliance\n   - Interpretability\n\n4. SOTA Comparison (Score 0.1-1.0):\n   - Performance potential\n   - Innovation aspects\n   - Technical advantages\n   - Biological accuracy\n   - Overall competitiveness\n\n5. Implementation Strategy (Score 0.1-1.0):\n   - Development approach\n   - Resource allocation\n   - Timeline realism\n   - Risk management\n   - Quality assurance\n\nProvide a detailed justification for each score and an overall score (0.1-1.0) weighted across all categories.\nFocus on potential for surpassing SOTA performance in single-cell perturbation prediction."
